WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
Welcome back to the David Pakman show.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:05.069
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Katherine Eban, who is an investigative journalist

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:06.390
and Fortune magazine

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:12.400
contributor, as well as the author of bottle
of lies, the inside story of the generic drug.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.400
Boom.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.450
Catherine, so great to talk to you today.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.690
Thank you so much for having me on the show.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:23.040
So generic drugs is an issue that relates
to intellectual property.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.090
It relates to FDA regulation, it relates to
the medical world.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:31.870
I mean, there's so many different areas that
are relevant to talking about generic drugs,

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:37.109
but I, I guess to begin with, how far back
does the concept of a generic go?

00:00:37.109 --> 00:00:50.359
Well, I mean, it really goes all the way back
to, uh, probably the 1920s, 1930s.

00:00:50.359 --> 00:01:00.429
But the idea that, um, you could, you could
basically be a company that breaks down a

00:01:00.429 --> 00:01:07.010
brand name drug and tries to remake version
of it is a very old concept

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.440
in the sort of modern generic drug world.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:16.580
When we look at generic alternatives to name
brand drugs, many people believe that it is

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:23.430
down to the finest level, the exact same thing
as the brand name, except without the brand

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.430
name.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:28.340
It turns out that, that that's not always
true and it's in fact often not true in ways

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.670
that are sort of concerning and dangerous.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.640
Talk a little bit about that,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:33.870
right.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:37.460
Well, it's actually almost never true.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:40.940
So a generics are not identical copies.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:47.410
They are versions of brand name drugs and
the FDA recognizes that they're not identical

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:54.010
because they give a range, uh, that the generic
can fall into in terms of the dissolution

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.910
of the drug in the blood.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:02.730
So under the best of circumstances, uh, it,
the generic is similar but not identical.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:09.819
Um, they may have different active dick, different
additional ingredients, different time release

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:10.819
mechanism.

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:15.079
They're made in different factories, uh, avail,
dissolve differently in the body.

00:02:15.079 --> 00:02:17.569
Uh, but where it really gets dangerous.

00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:23.360
Uh, and what I document in my book bottle
of lies is the ways in which a generic drug

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:30.489
manufacturing plants on many of which operate
overseas are actually committing fraud and

00:02:30.489 --> 00:02:35.000
submitting fake data to get approval from
the FDA to sell drugs.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Right.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.469
So there's sort of two layers to this.

00:02:37.469 --> 00:02:43.079
One is, is there even the expectation or belief
that the generic is in practical terms, the

00:02:43.079 --> 00:02:45.340
same thing as the name brand drug.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.889
Then separately from that, there's the question
of oversight and accuracy as to whether the

00:02:50.889 --> 00:02:52.620
generic is even what it purports to be.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.030
And it seems as though both of these are issues
that are happening at the same time.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:57.030
Yeah.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.030
Right.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:04.270
I mean, the, the idea of the generic as a
version a and not an identical copy is accepted

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.629
and actually acceptable.

00:03:06.629 --> 00:03:12.829
And for many, many drugs, uh, aversion is
fine, although there are some drugs were really

00:03:12.829 --> 00:03:16.700
precise, dosing is required and those can
be a problem.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:24.109
But the, I think the greater risk for American
consumers is that the FDA is not adequately

00:03:24.109 --> 00:03:28.060
policing these manufacturing plants overseas.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:35.400
And essentially accepting the data that these
companies are submitting as accurate a when

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.900
they are not.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.689
And it's not always, I mean, so we can, there
are a number of criticisms of the FDA, many

00:03:41.689 --> 00:03:43.400
of which I've made on this program.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.719
But is this a limitation in terms of the standards
that are being applied to generics?

00:03:47.719 --> 00:03:52.530
Is this something that the FDA is choosing
not to do, or is it something that it is,

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:56.340
at least in some cases unable to do?

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.340
Huh?

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:03.819
Well, I think the two are somewhat connected,
but basically the FDA is a agency that because

00:04:03.819 --> 00:04:11.109
of globalization was suddenly required to
police manufacturing plants all over the world.

00:04:11.109 --> 00:04:17.750
And it essentially acknowledges the difficulty
of this because for us, plants, when he inspects,

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:22.620
they just, the inspectors arrive on an unannounced
and stay as long as is needed.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:29.370
But because of the complex logistics of overseas
inspections, the FDA announces that it's arriving

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:35.110
to these plants, gives them weeks and sometimes
months of advanced notice that they're coming.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:41.770
So my investigation reveals a, they will send
in the plants, will send in data fabrication

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:48.770
teams to alter documents, invent documents,
uh, backdate documents and these are all,

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:56.500
you know, quality data quality documents that
speak to, uh, whether the drug is bio equivalent,

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.419
whether it's got impurities in it or not.

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:04.840
Um, all kinds of issues that are critical
to have the drug functions.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:10.080
When we think about this, there might be people
depending on their health circumstances, who

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:16.669
here is talking about this and they go to
Advil versus the cvs generic equivalent for

00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:17.669
example.

00:05:17.669 --> 00:05:22.830
But there are other people who may be in situations
where they are actually using generic alternatives

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:27.280
to very, very expensive name brand drugs depending
on the, the medical conditions that they may

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:28.340
have.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:33.669
Does your investigation suggests that the
same issues exist to the same degree with

00:05:33.669 --> 00:05:38.889
both the Advil generics and the really expensive
drugs for, for less common conditions?

00:05:38.889 --> 00:05:41.520
Or is it more one versus the other?

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.520
It's actually both.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:47.800
So, just to give you an example in the book,
I follow these cardiologists from the Cleveland

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:53.470
Clinic who realize that when their patients
were getting switched to certain generics,

00:05:53.470 --> 00:06:00.169
um, they were initially stable and then they
were no longer stable and even, uh, they found

00:06:00.169 --> 00:06:05.409
that heart transplant patients were suffering
organ rejection when they were switched to

00:06:05.409 --> 00:06:07.080
certain Indian generics.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:12.360
So there is a, uh, that's generic drugs that
were manufactured in India.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:16.509
Um, so there's, you know, life threatening
dangers here.

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:22.550
Um, my book focuses less on over the counter
drugs, but you know, what consumers should

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:30.250
know is that the drug store brands say Duane
Reed, cvs, the drug store chains are contracting

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:37.319
with the lowest cost Indian manufacturers
to make those over the counter drugs.

00:06:37.319 --> 00:06:42.870
And those plants are really, uh, audited and
inspected infrequently.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.360
So there is a quality issue there as well.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:50.669
So, I mean, having dug into this and investigated
it for the period of time that you did to,

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:57.120
to write the book is your takeaway that there's
just too much risk with any generic and that

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:01.660
patients should be doing everything they can
to get their insurance to cover the name brand.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:05.439
Because we'll talk about the sort of practical
limitations of that.

00:07:05.439 --> 00:07:09.389
I mean, are you, are you, did you come away
that concerned where you would just want the

00:07:09.389 --> 00:07:12.169
name brand of any drug that you're taking?

00:07:12.169 --> 00:07:15.180
Um, I would say two things about that.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:22.669
I came away extremely concerned and I interviewed
plenty of FDA investigators who themselves

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:26.169
would not take the drugs made in the plants
that they were inspecting.

00:07:26.169 --> 00:07:27.169
Hmm.

00:07:27.169 --> 00:07:34.020
Uh, but that said, I also recognize cost is
a serious reality and it is for me as well.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:41.389
So, um, what I do is I often ask my pharmacist
to switch from one generic, which I don't

00:07:41.389 --> 00:07:46.069
trust to another generic made by a different
company that I might trust more.

00:07:46.069 --> 00:07:47.110
That's it.

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:48.110
Yeah.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:49.199
It's knowable.

00:07:49.199 --> 00:07:55.810
Which brands, generics are, are more or less
is trustworthy even the right word?

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:56.810
Hmm.

00:07:56.810 --> 00:07:58.710
Um, it is a knowable thing.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:03.860
Now the problem is do you have to be an investigative
journalist who's looked into this for 10 years

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:11.599
to know it in order to help consumers, I have
put information on my website, which is Catherine

00:08:11.599 --> 00:08:12.849
even.com.

00:08:12.849 --> 00:08:20.210
Uh, and so there is information about steps
that they can take to try to figure out, um,

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:25.259
whether they're generic is trustworthy or
not and what the options are if they determine

00:08:25.259 --> 00:08:26.469
that they want to switch.

00:08:26.469 --> 00:08:29.449
Um, you know, it is a bit of a maze.

00:08:29.449 --> 00:08:34.370
I'm not going to be, you know, kind of sugarcoat
that.

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:40.219
I think what's really needed is congressional
action here to fix the problems that I've

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:41.320
exposed.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:45.510
But in lieu of that, I think there are certain
steps that consumers can take.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.800
There's also something called an authorized
generic.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:55.450
So that is a generic where the brand company
has made a contract with a generic company

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:58.050
handed over it's recipe.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:06.320
Um, and the generic company is making the
drug under a contract so it's actually closer

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:07.320
to the brand.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:11.910
And sometimes those authorized generics are
actually manufactured in the very same plants

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:13.010
as the brand name drug.

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:14.010
What, what?

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:19.110
You know a lot of people who have dealt with
insurance companies, no, is that depending

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:22.920
on the coverage that you have and depending
on where you get your insurance, whether an

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:28.060
individual marketplace, as a self employed
person or through a work connected insurance

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:33.639
plan, there are all sorts of complex rules
about what your insurance will or will not

00:09:33.639 --> 00:09:36.290
cover when it comes to prescription medications.

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:41.170
And doctors certainly have the ability to
put no substitution on prescriptions, which

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:44.740
collectively tells the pharmacy that a named
brand is what should be provided.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:49.190
But that does not guarantee that runs health
insurance is going to cover the name brand

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:51.430
at the same price that the generic would be
covered.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:55.959
So this is, I mean this is really complicated
stuff, particularly for people who, who require

00:09:55.959 --> 00:09:57.840
medications that are extremely,

00:09:57.840 --> 00:09:59.760
yeah, absolutely.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:05.280
This is not simple and you're quite right
that even with the do not substitute, there's

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:11.410
no guarantee that the insurance company will
cover a brand when a generic is available.

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:16.230
Can you talk a little bit about, I mean obviously
there are a number of different types of concerns

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:17.230
there.

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:18.490
There's straight up safety concerns, right?

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:21.700
Upside of Beck's that one could experience
experienced adverse reactions.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:28.970
There's also the concern of just a drug that
in June it's generic version is less potent

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:32.960
or may not achieve the same results as the
name brand.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:40.279
How would you sort of put percentages on those
two concerns and others that may exist as

00:10:40.279 --> 00:10:41.279
a general rule?

00:10:41.279 --> 00:10:45.110
When we talk about ways in which the generics
may be suboptimal?

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:46.110
Right?

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:48.370
So I don't know that I can offer percentages.

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:55.220
What I can say is that even if the generics
are fine, there is an issue for what are called

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:57.520
narrow therapeutic index drugs.

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:03.460
And so those are drugs that require really
precise dosing and the doctors who prescribed

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:10.930
those, so it's cardiologists, psychiatrists,
endocrinologist, they are, um, familiar with

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:20.399
this as a problem and they have raised questions
about whether the FDA standards, um, are narrow

00:11:20.399 --> 00:11:28.030
enough to make, to ensure that the generics
are clinically equivalent to the brand.

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:31.519
So that's like in one basket of concerns.

00:11:31.519 --> 00:11:39.790
Um, unfortunately with this other basket,
um, there is really widespread fraud in the

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:43.200
overseas manufacturing plants that are making
the majority of our generics.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:49.350
So just as an example, um, I feature in the
book an Fda investigator named Peter Baker

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:56.660
who became exceptionally good at finding data
fraud in these plants.

00:11:56.660 --> 00:12:03.310
And over five years he investigated 86 plants
in India and China and found some element

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:09.350
of data fraud or data deception in about four
fifths of those plants.

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:11.230
So a stunning number.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.350
Yeah, it really is.

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:14.390
Yeah, absolutely.

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:20.649
So, I mean, these are, these are plants that
are doing everything from using the brand

00:12:20.649 --> 00:12:29.250
name drugs, uh, to run tests and take that
data and submit it to the FDA as though it

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:32.220
was their own data from their own drugs.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:39.750
So completely supplanting the data, inventing
documents and steaming them in rooms overnight

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.779
to make them look old.

00:12:41.779 --> 00:12:49.010
Um, you know, completely just discarding critical
manufacturing documents where FDA investigators

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:52.890
have dug them out of dumpsters when documents
are supposed to be retained.

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:59.850
So, you know, these are really, um, major
lapses in what are called good manufacturing

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:02.750
practices that these plants have to follow.

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:08.270
I mean, some plants have, even when you have
a sterile manufacturing plant, and that's

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:12.870
very serious, they have to follow a sort of
a septic regulations.

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:17.310
Um, they have literally invented there sterility
data.

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:23.440
They're supposed to test air water surfaces
to make sure they're free of microbial contaminants

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:25.529
and they've just invented that data.

00:13:25.529 --> 00:13:27.430
They're not really running the tests.

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:32.060
Uh, so that's the kind of things that are
going on in these plants.

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:33.940
Absolutely, absolutely stunning.

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:40.190
Um, make sure to check out Katherine's book,
bottle of lies, the inside story of the generic

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:41.190
drug.

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:42.190
Boom.

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:43.589
We have been speaking with the book's author,
Katherine Eban.

00:13:43.589 --> 00:13:44.589
Yeah.

00:13:44.589 --> 00:13:47.199
Who is an investigative journalist and Fortune
magazine contributor.

00:13:47.199 --> 00:13:50.180
Katherine, so great to talk to you about this.

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:51.180
Great to talk to you.

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:52.180
Thank you so much.

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:52.184
